{"title":"Coplanar","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>In geometry, a set of points, lines, or 2-dimensional polygons are coplanar if and only if they are on the same plane.\n3 points are always coplanar, and if the 3 points are distinct and non-collinear, they define a unique plane\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/3/4a369c96d3017021c4966cb5f33bde9170fba2fb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\textbf{Coplanar Points in Geometry}}\\]\" width=\"347\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/b/d9bc9acee828a3b7d2805f439b544cabb6733063.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\text{A set of points is said to be coplanar if all the points lie on the same plane, where a plane is a flat two-dimensional surface extending infinitely in all directions.}}\\]\" width=\"1636\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/7/3b7c3f1a3de024e9494cd7318de00af903049664.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\textbf{Basic Properties: } \\text{Any three points are always coplanar since exactly one plane passes through three non-collinear points, while four or more points may or may not be coplanar depending on whether they lie on the same plane.}}\\]\" width=\"2355\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/1/6d1c86a3bbc4ac59eeac08efefdb666145c3fa06.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\text{If four points lie in the same plane, they are coplanar; otherwise, they are non-coplanar and can form a three-dimensional figure such as a tetrahedron.}}\\]\" width=\"1546\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/9/fe9e70691a370abbd2dc37ade619fc00d209ab4a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\textbf{Line Relationships: } \\text{Two intersecting lines are always coplanar, two parallel lines are always coplanar, whereas skew lines are non-coplanar since they do not lie in the same plane and do not intersect.}}\\]\" width=\"2072\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/7/6a754c2fd5339a38414e8415c5d7332b78fab6ac.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\textbf{Coordinate Condition: } \\text{Let } A(x_1,y_1,z_1),\\ B(x_2,y_2,z_2),\\ C(x_3,y_3,z_3),\\ D(x_4,y_4,z_4). \\text{ The points are coplanar if and only if}}\\]\" width=\"1143\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/b/e9b0740ce9937573d75596150bfc18317e4a5b2b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\begin{vmatrix} x_2 - x_1 &amp; y_2 - y_1 &amp; z_2 - z_1 \\ x_3 - x_1 &amp; y_3 - y_1 &amp; z_3 - z_1 \\ x_4 - x_1 &amp; y_4 - y_1 &amp; z_4 - z_1 \\end{vmatrix} = 0}\\]\" width=\"798\" height=\"33\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/7/eb769768ab9aaf2c8903fc7493c1a476b098d326.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\text{which means the scalar triple product is zero and hence the volume of the tetrahedron formed by the four points is zero.}}\\]\" width=\"1234\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2be8df7812fb8d93acaa9f11ca33ba163c850f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\textbf{Geometric Insight: } \\text{Coplanarity implies that a three-dimensional configuration reduces to a two-dimensional structure, which is extremely useful in simplifying geometric reasoning and proofs.}}\\]\" width=\"1987\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/2/85257e6923feea812dfce889893885314456a571.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\textbf{Applications: } \\text{Coplanarity is used to determine whether points lie on the same surface, to simplify 3D problems into 2D, to analyze line relationships, and in vector geometry and plane equations.}}\\]\" width=\"2018\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/2/1325bc8d678e7aeb6a32ebd2469d1fbbdcd71305.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{\\Large \\textbf{Conclusion: } \\text{Coplanar points form the foundation of planar geometry and are essential for connecting two-dimensional and three-dimensional concepts.}}\\]\" width=\"1563\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=~Trashboycan\" title=\"~Trashboycan\">~Trashboycan</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018081645\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.008 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.018 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 34/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3625/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:14963:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018081645 and revision id 281535. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}